Scientific breakthroughs in data-driven science increasingly rely on applying sophisticated AI models to comprehensive, discipline-specific datasets. However, the computational resources required to evaluate every possible model against a large dataset are excessively expensive, rendering them impractical to execute. As a result, existing benchmarking approaches utilize select, representative datasets, but this limited method restricts extensive analysis and introduces bias towards models that perform well on those chosen datasets, thus overlooking their performance on others. In response to these challenges, we propose a general profiling framework that allows for in-depth analysis of models on a wide range of datasets of any size, provided the datasets can be efficiently divided into smaller, homogeneous sections. Using the example of image classification, we demonstrate how our framework operates on a particular dataset and then is adapted for use on other, distinct datasets through a simple, automated process. Ultimately, we show that models with strong results on one dataset do not always perform well on another, and that our framework offers a more accurate and comprehensive assessment. It also enables the training of models on one dataset while evaluating them on another, thereby producing more resilient models. Additionally, our framework makes it more difficult to manipulate dataset labels and creates secure benchmarks. Lastly, we highlight theoretical aspects that could affect the creation of our benchmark and propose future improvements. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/dramdam/dataset-particular-profiling. For space considerations, references are listed at the end of the text. 

\textit{In this work, we introduce a comprehensive evaluation procedure for training AI models on specific datasets. Unlike existing, limited approaches, our method allows for in-depth analysis of models within a wide range of datasets, and we show that through adaptation, it can efficiently handle large datasets. Notably, our method enables the training of models on one dataset by evaluating them on another, thereby producing more resilient models. We also highlight theoretical aspects that could affect our benchmark and propose future improvements.}

\textit{This work is inspired by a question about how to evaluate AI models effectively, and through a practical example, we develop a general profiling framework that allows for in-depth analysis of models on a wide range of datasets. We theorize that this adaptation will influence the development of future AI models.}

\textit{We present a profiling framework that allows for in-depth analysis of models on a wide range of datasets, and we show through different examples that this method could be efficient on various tasks. We speculate that this method will be particularly useful within the context of distributed training.}

